{
  "requirement": "A definition glob MUST match a complete full name. `*` MUST match zero or more characters, including dots; all other valid name characters MUST match literally. An empty glob MUST be invalid. Every definition selection MUST use this matching rule, including requirements evidence inputs, CLI definition filters, and the authoring `references` tool, and MUST match against both requirements and named examples.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check `requirements.references.definition_globs`. Every selection path calls the shared `crate::definition_glob::validate` and `matches`. I confirmed this in `src/authoring/inputs.rs`, `src/authoring/tools.rs` (`fn[references]` and `fn[referencing]`), `src/commands.rs` (status and info) and `src/inputs.rs` (`InputSelector::resolve`). So the matching rule needs direct tests, and each consumer needs at least one test that exercises it.\n\nObligations and the tests that cover them:\n- **Complete full-name match.** `src/definition_glob.rs#mod[tests]/fn[wildcard_crosses_dot_segments_and_matches_the_whole_name]` rejects both a prefixed name and a suffixed name for `foo.*.bar`. It also checks that `foo.bar.*` does not match `foo.bar`.\n- **`*` matches zero or more characters, including dots.** The same unit test matches `foo.*.bar` against both `foo.x.bar` and `foo.x.y.bar`. In `tests/commands.rs#fn[commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors]`, the glob `requirements.*ready` matches `requirements.ready` (zero characters) and `requirements.xready` (one character), and it excludes `requirements.pending`. In `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirements_inputs_track_matched_declarations_in_full_name_order]`, `foo.*.bar` matches the nested name `foo.a.b.bar`.\n- **Other characters match literally.** `.` is checked as literal (`foo.bar` does not match `fooxbar`), as are `_` and `-`. The unit test asserts `foo_bar-baz` matches itself and does not match `fooXbar-baz` or `foo_barXbaz`.\n- **An empty glob is invalid.** The unit test asserts `validate(\"\")` is an error. The evidence-inputs test asserts the error \"definition glob must not be empty\". The authoring `references` test rejects an empty selection string.\n- **Invalid characters are rejected.** `foo[bar]` is rejected in the unit test. `[` is rejected for the evidence inputs and for the CLI `info` and `status` commands (\"invalid definition glob\").\n- **Applies to requirements evidence inputs.** The evidence test checks glob matching, the input's matches list, the \"matches no requirement or example\" error, and add/remove tracking. `tests/definitions.rs#fn[selections_match_requirements_and_examples_but_never_named_definitions]` also covers this path.\n- **Applies to CLI definition filters.** The `status` and `info` commands are tested with `rules.*` and with `requirements.*ready` (the `requirements.*ready` check is in the status test). Unmatched globs fail with \"no definitions match\".\n- **Applies to the authoring `references` tool.** `references_list_names_and_flattened_sites_without_recording_inputs` tests `requirements.*` (which flattens sites) and an exact name. It rejects the missing name `requirements.missing`, a bad name and an empty selection. The authoring `habeas` requirements read, `ripgrep` referencing and `ast_grep` referencing tests also use globs.\n- **Matches both requirements and named examples.** The glob `requirements.*` returns both `requirements.case` (an example) and `requirements.ready`. The `rules.*` CLI and evidence tests also include the example.\n\nI found no plausible violating behavior that these tests would accept. The only thing not tested directly is the CLI with an empty glob. That path uses the same `validate` function, which the unit and evidence tests cover.",
  "references": "288667bf2256bfb2c1e3d9a893df50df3abc797b6e7682916392f4e0759318ed",
